          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Types of Exterior Caulk: Which Chemistry Is Right for Your Joint?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best exterior caulk</strong> depends on how much the joint moves, whether it will be painted, and what substrates it connects. Here are the four chemistries that matter for exterior work:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Siliconized Acrylic Latex Sealants</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>DAP Dynaflex 230</strong> and <strong>DAP Alex Plus</strong> blend acrylic latex with silicone additives, delivering meaningful flexibility (25-55% joint movement depending on the formula) while remaining fully <strong>paintable</strong> and cleaning up with water. This is the <strong>best paintable exterior caulk</strong> category and the right default for trim, siding seams, and window and door frames that will be painted to match the surrounding surface. Best for: general trim and siding work where paintability is required.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">100% Silicone Sealants</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>GE Max Shield</strong> are pure silicone — no acrylic or latex content — and deliver the best raw waterproofing, UV resistance, and joint movement (up to 100%) of any caulk chemistry. The trade-off is absolute: <strong>100% silicone cannot be painted</strong>, ever, under any circumstances. Best for: window glazing, metal flashing seams, gutter joints, and any gap that will remain unpainted permanently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acrylic Elastomeric Sealants</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Sashco Big Stretch</strong> represents this category — acrylic-based but engineered for extreme elongation (up to 500%), far beyond standard siliconized latex. It's fully paintable and remains flexible in extreme cold. This is the <strong>best exterior caulk for high-movement joints</strong> like log home checking, wide gaps between dissimilar materials, and older homes with significant seasonal racking. Best for: log homes, wide trim gaps, and joints with heavy seasonal movement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Advanced Polymer Hybrid Sealants</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>OSI Quad Advanced Formula</strong> use proprietary hybrid polymer chemistry to combine silicone-like flexibility and multi-substrate adhesion with full paintability. They tend to bond to the widest range of materials — wood, vinyl, metal, masonry, and glass — in a single product. Best for: window and door installation, new siding jobs, and any joint spanning multiple materials.
          </p>

