          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. DAP Dynaflex 230 vs. Sashco Big Stretch vs. GE Max Shield: Head-to-Head Comparison
          </h2>

          <p>
            These three products anchor the three main strategies for exterior sealing, so we tested them head-to-head on comparable joints. Here's exactly how they compare — because the <strong>siliconized latex vs. silicone vs. elastomeric</strong> debate has a clear, use-case-specific answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Movement Capability</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>GE Max Shield</strong> wins outright on rated joint movement at up to 100%, followed by <strong>Sashco Big Stretch</strong> at roughly 500% elongation (a different but related measurement of stretch-and-recover capability), with <strong>DAP Dynaflex 230</strong> rated for 55% movement. For a joint that genuinely moves a lot — log home checking, wide gaps between dissimilar materials — Big Stretch's paintable elastomeric formula usually wins because it combines huge stretch with the ability to be color-matched.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paintability</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Dynaflex 230</strong> and <strong>Big Stretch</strong> are both fully paintable within 30 minutes to an hour. <strong>Max Shield</strong> cannot be painted under any circumstances — its silicone chemistry permanently repels paint. If the joint needs to match a painted surface, Max Shield is disqualified regardless of its superior waterproofing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Waterproofing & UV Resistance</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Max Shield</strong> wins decisively here — 100% silicone simply does not absorb water or chalk under UV exposure the way acrylic-based products eventually will, even premium ones. <strong>Dynaflex 230</strong> and <strong>Big Stretch</strong> both perform very well in this category for a paintable product, but neither matches pure silicone's raw, decades-long waterproofing performance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost Comparison</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>DAP Dynaflex 230</strong>: ~$6-8 per 10.1 oz tube, covers 25-30 linear feet of a 1/4-inch bead. <strong>Sashco Big Stretch</strong>: ~$9-11 per 10.5 oz tube — the priciest of the three, reflecting its extreme elongation formula. <strong>GE Max Shield</strong>: ~$7-9 per 10.1 oz tube. For general trim and siding work, Dynaflex 230 is the most cost-effective choice. Big Stretch's premium is worth paying only when the joint genuinely needs that much stretch.
          </p>

