        <div id="how-to-apply-caulk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Apply Exterior Caulk: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Caulking your own exterior joints is one of the most cost-effective DIY maintenance tasks a homeowner can do, often taking a single weekend and saving <strong>$300-900</strong> compared to hiring a painter or handyman for the same job. The process is simple, but <strong>surface preparation and joint sizing</strong> determine whether the seal lasts a decade or fails within a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Remove the Old Caulk</h3>
          <p>
            Use a utility knife or a dedicated caulk-removal tool to score along both edges of the existing bead, then pull it out in strips. Aged silicone caulk can be stubborn — a caulk softener gel or a heat gun on low setting will loosen it without damaging the surrounding paint or trim. Remove every trace of old material; new caulk will not bond reliably to old caulk residue.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Clean and Dry the Joint</h3>
          <p>
            Wipe the joint with denatured alcohol or a mild detergent solution to remove dust, mildew, chalking paint residue, and old adhesive. Let the joint dry completely — most sealants, especially silicone, need a dry surface for proper adhesion. On masonry joints, brush away any loose mortar dust or efflorescence before proceeding.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Size the Gap and Use Backer Rod</h3>
          <p>
            For gaps wider than 1/4 inch, insert closed-cell foam <strong>backer rod</strong> before caulking. Backer rod limits the caulk's contact to two points (the two sides of the joint) rather than three (both sides plus the bottom), which allows the bead to stretch properly without tearing at the base — a principle sealant manufacturers call the "hourglass" bead profile. Skipping backer rod on wide gaps is one of the most common reasons exterior caulk fails prematurely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Cut the Tip and Apply the Bead</h3>
          <p>
            Cut the tube tip at a 45-degree angle sized to the joint width — a smaller opening for hairline gaps, a larger one for wider joints. Load the tube into a caulk gun and apply steady, even pressure while drawing the gun along the joint in one continuous motion, rather than stopping and starting, which causes uneven beads and air pockets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Tool the Bead</h3>
          <p>
            Immediately after applying the bead, tool it with a wet finger, a dedicated caulk tool, or a rounded popsicle stick to press the sealant into the joint and create a smooth, slightly concave finish. Tooling matters more than most DIYers realize — it forces the caulk into full contact with both substrate surfaces, eliminating the air gaps that cause early adhesion failure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 6: Let It Cure Before Painting or Weather Exposure</h3>
          <p>
            Most sealants skin over and become paintable within 30 minutes to an hour, but check the specific product's rating — Sashco Big Stretch, for example, needs about 24 hours before it's ready to be worked further. Where possible, avoid caulking immediately before a forecasted rainstorm; give the bead at least a few hours of dry weather to begin curing before it faces driving rain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Buying Considerations -->
        <div id="buying-considerations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Best Exterior Caulk by Climate and Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best exterior caulk</strong> for your project depends heavily on where you live and what materials you're sealing. Here's how to match the product to your specific situation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Climates and Freeze-Thaw Cycling</h3>
          <p>
            In climates with hard winters, prioritize sealants rated for extreme low temperatures and strong flexibility retention in the cold. <strong>Sashco Big Stretch</strong>, rated to -40°F, is a standout here, and <strong>DAP Dynaflex 230</strong>'s -20°F rating covers most northern climates comfortably. A caulk that goes brittle in cold weather will crack at exactly the moment a joint is contracting the most — the worst possible failure mode.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot, High-UV Climates</h3>
          <p>
            Southern and high-altitude climates with intense, prolonged UV exposure benefit from products with the strongest UV resistance. <strong>GE Max Shield</strong>'s 100% silicone formula resists chalking and degradation better than any acrylic-based product over the long term, making it the top choice for unpainted joints in intense-sun climates. For painted joints, <strong>OSI Quad Advanced</strong>'s 50-year durability rating reflects strong UV-resistant hybrid polymer chemistry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood Siding and Log Homes</h3>
          <p>
            Wood, especially log construction, expands, contracts, and checks (develops small surface cracks) more than most other siding materials as it responds to humidity and temperature. This is squarely <strong>Sashco Big Stretch</strong>'s territory, with its 500% elongation and wood-toned color options that blend into log and timber surfaces without needing paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vinyl and Fiber Cement Siding</h3>
          <p>
            Vinyl siding expands and contracts significantly with temperature, more than wood or fiber cement, so joints where vinyl meets trim need real flexibility. <strong>DAP Dynaflex 230</strong> or <strong>OSI Quad Advanced</strong> both handle this movement well while remaining paintable to match trim color.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brick, Stone, and Masonry</h3>
          <p>
            Masonry is porous, alkaline, and often slightly dusty at the surface, which causes poor adhesion in caulks not formulated for it. <strong>Geocel 2300</strong> is purpose-built for this substrate, with masonry-matched colors and strong adhesion to brick, stone, stucco, and concrete without a primer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Windows, Glass, and Metal Flashing</h3>
          <p>
            Glass-to-frame joints and metal flashing seams see significant thermal expansion in metal and benefit from maximum flexibility and waterproofing rather than paintability. <strong>GE Max Shield</strong>'s 100% silicone formula and up-to-100% joint movement rating make it the clear choice for these unpainted, high-performance joints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
        <PlanTheJob />

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for exterior use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DAP Dynaflex 230 Premium Elastomeric Sealant is the best overall exterior caulk. It handles up to 55% joint movement, is paintable in 30 minutes, remains flexible from -20°F to 140°F, and is backed by a 10-year durability guarantee. For high-movement joints like log homes, Sashco Big Stretch (500% elongation) is a better fit. For joints that will never be painted, GE Max Shield's 100% silicone offers superior waterproofing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between silicone and acrylic latex exterior caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                100% silicone caulk, like GE Max Shield, offers superior waterproofing, UV resistance, and joint movement (up to 100%), but it cannot be painted under any circumstances. Siliconized acrylic latex caulk, like DAP Dynaflex 230 or DAP Alex Plus, blends some of silicone's flexibility with full paintability and easy water cleanup. For trim, siding, and any joint you plan to paint, choose siliconized acrylic latex. For window glazing, metal flashing, or joints that will remain unpainted, choose 100% silicone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over exterior silicone caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. True 100% silicone caulk, such as GE Max Shield, will not accept paint under any circumstances, even after sanding or priming — the silicone surface actively repels paint. If a joint needs to be painted, use a siliconized acrylic latex sealant like DAP Dynaflex 230, DAP Alex Plus, or a hybrid polymer sealant like OSI Quad Advanced instead, all of which are formulated to accept paint within about 30 minutes of application.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much joint movement can exterior caulk handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product's chemistry. Standard acrylic latex caulks handle roughly 25-35% joint movement, suitable for stable trim and siding gaps. Premium siliconized acrylic latex sealants like DAP Dynaflex 230 handle up to 55%. Dedicated elastomeric sealants like Sashco Big Stretch can handle around 500% elongation, making them ideal for log homes and joints with significant seasonal movement. True 100% silicone sealants like GE Max Shield can handle up to 100% joint movement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What caulk should I use for brick or masonry gaps?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Geocel 2300 Trimmable Sealant is formulated specifically for brick, stone, stucco, and concrete, with strong adhesion to porous masonry surfaces and masonry-matched colors like mortar gray and tan. It also features freeze-cure technology allowing application down to 30°F, which is useful for masonry repair projects in cooler weather. OSI Quad Advanced is also a strong option for joints where masonry meets wood or vinyl trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does exterior caulk take to cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most exterior caulks are tack-free or paintable within 30 minutes to an hour, but full cure — when the sealant reaches its final flexibility and strength — typically takes 7 to 30 days depending on the product, temperature, and humidity. DAP Dynaflex 230 can take up to 30 days for full cure in cool, humid conditions. Geocel 2300 is trimmable in about an hour. Avoid exposing fresh caulk to rain or extreme temperatures during the first 24-48 hours whenever possible.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I remove old caulk before applying new exterior caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a utility knife or dedicated caulk-removal tool to score and cut away old caulk along both edges of the joint, then pull it out in strips. A caulk softener or heat gun can help loosen stubborn or aged silicone caulk. After removing old material, clean the joint with denatured alcohol or a mild detergent to remove residue, mildew, and old adhesive, then let it dry completely before applying fresh caulk. Skipping this step is the most common reason new caulk fails to bond.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is exterior caulk waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when properly applied, all of the exterior caulks in this guide are waterproof once cured. 100% silicone products like GE Max Shield become waterproof fastest, in as little as 30 minutes. Siliconized acrylic latex products like DAP Dynaflex 230 and hybrid polymers like OSI Quad Advanced are also fully waterproof once cured, typically within 30 minutes to an hour of skinning over, though full cure and maximum flexibility take longer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

