        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Exterior Caulking Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior caulk lives on a joint that moves with every temperature swing. Most premature failures come from asking a sealant to do something its chemistry or its joint geometry will not allow.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Interior Acrylic Outdoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Painter's caulk is designed for static interior joints and has almost no movement capability or UV resistance. On an exterior joint it goes brittle within a season and splits down the middle. Exterior work needs a siliconised acrylic at minimum, and polyurethane or hybrid polymer where movement is real.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking Over a Wet or Dirty Joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealants bond to clean, dry, sound surfaces and to nothing else. Old caulk residue, dust, chalking paint, and moisture all prevent adhesion, and the bead peels away cleanly from one side within months. Cut out the old material, brush the joint clean, and let it dry fully.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Three-Sided Adhesion in a Deep Joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A caulk bead that sticks to the back of the joint as well as both sides cannot stretch, so it tears when the joint opens. Deep joints need backer rod pushed in first, so the sealant bonds to two faces only and can flex between them. This is the most-ignored rule in caulking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Joints That Need to Drain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Not every gap should be closed. The bottom edge of siding, weep holes in brickwork, and the base of window trim are drainage paths, and caulking them traps water inside the wall. Seal the top and sides of trim, and leave the bottom open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">What is the best exterior caulk for a tight budget?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Loctite Polyseamseal All Purpose Sealant is the best budget exterior caulk, typically costing 40-50% less per tube than premium options while still offering paintability and adequate flexibility for stable, low-movement joints. For higher-movement or high-sun joints, spending more on DAP Dynaflex 230 or Sashco Big Stretch will pay off in fewer callbacks and longer service life.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

